**Idea.** Keep a map `eventType -> lastTriggeredTime`. When an event arrives at time `t`:
- if the type was never seen, or `t - last >= delay`, accept it, store `t`, return `true`;
- otherwise return `false` **without updating** the stored time (a rejected event must not extend the window).

Check the example with delay 5: shoot@1 accepted (last=1); shoot@2: 2-1=1 < 5, ignored; reload@3 accepted (independent type); shoot@6: 6-1=5 >= 5, accepted. Output `[true, false, true, true]` matches, which also confirms the boundary is inclusive.

```java
class EventThrottler {
    private final long delay;
    private final Map<String, Long> last = new HashMap<>();
    EventThrottler(long delay) { this.delay = delay; }

    boolean shouldTrigger(String type, long now) {
        Long prev = last.get(type);
        if (prev == null || now - prev >= delay) {
            last.put(type, now);
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

Time and space are O(1) per call and O(distinct types) overall. Pass `now` as a parameter (or an injected clock) so tests are deterministic. In production prefer a monotonic clock such as `System.nanoTime()` over wall time.

**Thread safety.** The check-then-put is a race: two threads could both pass the check. Options:
- Mark `shouldTrigger` `synchronized`; simple and correct, though all event types contend on one lock.
- Use `ConcurrentHashMap` and do the decision atomically with `compute`:

```java
boolean[] ok = {false};
last.compute(type, (k, prev) -> {
    if (prev == null || now - prev >= delay) { ok[0] = true; return now; }
    return prev;
});
return ok[0];
```

Per-key atomicity means different event types do not block each other.

**Extensions.** Per-event-type delays (a config map), per-player throttlers, removing idle entries to bound memory, and token-bucket or sliding-window rate limiting if you need "N per window" instead of "one per window".
